put('satr-02','Determine whether the eight retrieval cues can be reduced without removing a distinct condition for action.','The cue index contains superficially related pairs about windows, scheduling and prerequisites. Those similarities invite a smaller index, but the current arrangement already ties each cue to a different operational question. I do not yet have evidence that any is dispensable.',f'''AREA: the existing eight external retrieval cues, with particular attention to the apparent overlap of access/time conditions. BELONGS TO: immediate external-memory support. SATURATION SIGNAL: shared words such as window, before and schedule. The object is later cue selection, not the fixed exposure quota.

| Existing item | Purpose | Logged usage | Boundary that must survive |
|---|---|---|---|
{rows}

TOTAL COUNT: 8. EXPECTED COUNT under the actual next-use question inventory: 8 distinct conditions. RATIO: 1.0. No external population benchmark exists for the proper number of cues; this is a coverage-derived count for this exact index.

Candidate cluster A, continuous-window/location-window: the first compares one uninterrupted duration against the largest usable block; the second adds travel, setup, cleanup and buffer before an access deadline. A 12-minute task in 6/6/10 fails the first. A 20-minute task in a 30-minute room window fails the second when travel5+setup4+cleanup2 gives31. Merging into 'find enough time' loses which arithmetic to perform.

Candidate cluster B, future-release/deadline-before-mean: the first reserves a known future nonpreemptive interval; the second chooses deadlines over average wait among currently available tasks. The two-job release1 case changes whether the processor should be idle; the all-release0 four-job case changes which ready job goes first. Same scheduling vocabulary, different action.

Candidate cluster C, offline-material/baseline-exposure/criterion-first: acquiring readable content before losing network access, recording an unexposed baseline before exposure, and precommitting a changed test before feedback protect different required states. Offline possession does not establish a baseline; a baseline does not fix the later comparator; comparator precommitment does not provide a readable file.

Shared-resource is unique: logical independence can coexist with an unsafe common overwrite. No other cue supplies that resource constraint. Operational redundancy rate after those comparisons: 0/8 on the specified questions. Surface-word overlap is not counted as functional duplication.

LOW usage applies to four exact-key lookups; the other four are NEVER only within that finite four-query log. No actual human cue-use history exists. The strongest pruning argument is that the unqueried entries have not yet earned repeated use. It does not show that their action roles are redundant, and deleting them would remove explicit answers for four currently defined questions. Keeping eight short entries has unknown human review cost; no invented minutes are assigned.

CONSOLIDATE: none. PRUNE: none. KEEP AS-IS: all eight, with answer, exception and source intact. RESULT: 8 → 8, 0% reduction. The index remains unchanged. This does not rerun memy or claim delayed retention. SATR-01's narrower repeat list does not license pruning this different resource.''','The attempted reduction failed: no operationally redundant cue was found, so the conditions agent retained the eight-entry index.','The comparison protects distinct action conditions, but it repeats the already useful arrangement and earns no new useful-change credit.','REJECT','Question → answer → exception → source remains preferable to three broad labels (time, order, memory), because the latter cannot select the observed different operations.','Observe actual later cue use if available; test a cue whose answer fails on a changed access condition; distinguish deleting a cue from reducing repeated practice prompts.','No numerical 8x floor. All five stages cover all eight actual cues, three tested overlap clusters, the exact finite usage log and an explicit no-pruning decision.',later_use='uf-01 preserves separate release, dependency, access and resource switch conditions rather than merging them into one scheduling label.')
